   "id": "t11-fresh",
   "stage": "fresh",
   "text": "Quantization-aware training (QAT) keeps the PTQ (Post-Training Quantization) encoding for some layers because of the following reasons:\n\n1. **Accuracy Preservation**: Some layers, particularly those with high sensitivity to quantization errors, may benefit from using the PTQ encoding instead of the QAT encoding. This is because the PTQ encoding is derived from the actual quantized weights and activations, which are more representative of the quantized model's behavior.\n\n2. **Computational Efficiency**: Using the PTQ encoding can reduce the computational overhead associated with QAT, as it avoids the need for additional training iterations or complex operations during the quantization process.\n\n3. **Model Stability**: In some cases, the PTQ encoding can help maintain the stability of the model during the quantization process, especially when dealing with layers that have a high dynamic range or are prone to quantization errors.\n\n4. **Hybrid Quantization**: Some models use a hybrid approach where certain layers are quantized using QAT while others are quantized using PTQ. This can help balance the trade-off between accuracy and performance.\n\n5. **Layer-Specific Requirements**: Different layers may have different requirements for quantization. For example, convolutional layers might benefit from QAT, while fully connected layers might benefit from PTQ.\n\nIn summary, keeping the PTQ encoding for some layers can help improve the accuracy, stability, and efficiency of the quantized model.",
